      feeNote: "Fees are U of T's official 2025–26 totals (tuition + incidental fees, including UHIP health insurance). Year 1 Arts & Science students — including Computer Science, Rotman Commerce, Life Sciences and Social Sciences — pay the common Arts & Science rate; ⚠️ differentiation starts in Year 2: published UTSC figures show Computer Science rising to $72,480 and Management to $78,160 in Year 2+, while other Arts & Science programmes rise to $68,750. Engineering tuition alone is $70,060 (2025–26) before incidental fees.",
      english: "<strong>Holistic elements:</strong> Most U of T programmes decide on academic results alone, but ⚠️ Engineering requires a mandatory Online Student Profile from every applicant, and Rotman Commerce and Computer Science require a Supplemental Application (Architecture, Music and Kinesiology have their own supplementary or audition requirements).<br>English: U of T publishes NO exemption based on a CBSE/ISC English mark, and India is not on its list of countries that may qualify for exemption — if your first language is not English, plan on a test: IELTS Academic 6.5 overall with no band below 6.0; TOEFL iBT 89 with 22 in Speaking and Writing; PTE Academic 65 with no part below 60; Duolingo 120 with at least 120 in Production. IB English A (HL or SL) grade 4, A/AS Level English grade C, or GCSE/IGCSE English grade B (6) also meet the standard. A senior-level English course is additionally required for admission to every programme.",
      scholarships: "• <b>Lester B. Pearson International Scholarship</b> — U of T's flagship international award: covers tuition, books, incidental fees and full residence support for four years. No numeric minimum is published — selection is on outstanding academic achievement, creativity and leadership within your school. You cannot apply directly: your school must nominate you (one nominee per school), then you apply to U of T and complete the Pearson application via a private link.<br>• <b>University of Toronto Scholars Program</b> — about 900 awards of $10,000 for the first year (non-renewable); outstanding domestic and international students are automatically considered on their admission average. No numeric minimum published; no separate application.<br>• <b>President's Scholars of Excellence Program</b> — about 150 students receive a $15,000 first-year entrance scholarship (non-renewable); automatic consideration on the admission average.<br>• <b>International Scholar Award — U of T Scarborough</b> — $45,000 or $25,000, renewable for three additional years; international students applying from secondary school are automatically considered (Scarborough campus programmes).<br>• <b>U of T Engineering admission scholarships</b> — all Engineering applicants are automatically considered for Faculty admission scholarships on academic excellence and extracurricular involvement shown in the Online Student Profile.",
